th pkg/format and pkg/sys are prerequisites for du, but sys is potentially needed sooner for cat. However, since format comes first alphabetically and is more self-contained with just three capabilities and no platform-specific complexity, I'll go with that as my proposal.\n\nNow I need to write the task description, which means anchoring it to specific PRD requirements by ID as P6 requires. The challenge is I can't read the PRD file directly, but I can reference it in required_reading and let the stitch agent pull the actual requirement IDs when implementing.\n\nLooking at the code_status data, I can see the spec is marked complete for the foundational infrastructure batch, including the format use case. So I'll reference both the PRD and the use case specification in required_reading, then describe what needs to be implemented based on the architecture. The stitch agent will map these to the actual requirement IDs from the PRD when they read it.\n\nFor the task itself, I'm targeting the pkg/format/ implementation, and I need to figure out the correct PRD file path based on the naming conventions I'm seeing.\n\nSince the generation pipeline only targets cmd/ and pkg/ directories, and looking at what's already been started, format is the first alphabetically among the remaining 00.0 tasks. The architecture specifies that pkg/format/ should handle column alignment for tabular output, ANSI color codes for file types and permissions, and human-readable size formatting.\n\nI'm working through the file structure now—there's a question about naming conventions when there's no single primary exported type, but the constraint about splitting implementation and tests into separate tasks means I might need to break this into multiple task proposals if the format package has more than 4 PRD sub-requirements across its 3 main capabilities.\n\nSince du needs size formatting sooner than ls needs colors and alignment, I should prioritize that. Looking at the line counts, the three