ance criteria cover compilation without warnings, correct byte-to-unit conversions with appropriate precision, proper ANSI escape sequences, correct padding for variable-width columns, and documentation for all exported functions.\n\nI'm second-guessing whether to include doc comments given the constraint about not adding unnecessary documentation, but for a public API package this is standard Go practice and actually required by golangci-lint, so it belongs in the acceptance criteria. Now I need to finalize the task definition, though I realize I should derive the actual file names from the architecture rather than making assumptions. I need to name the files after their primary exported types rather than the package name, so `size.go` for a `Size` type, `color.go` for a `Color` type, and `column.go` for a `Column` type—these align with the architecture description and what the stitch agent will likely generate from the PRD. I'm skipping a `doc.go` file since it's unnecessary for a small package, and now I'm ready to write the final YAML, though I should double-check the constraint about PRD sub-sections.\n\nLooking at the requirements again, I have four items but \"Clean public API\" feels more like a general quality goal than a specific PRD requirement, so I'll trim it down to three focused requirements: human-readable size formatting, ANSI color codes, and column alignment. That keeps me well within the stated limit of at most 4 PRD sub-requirements, even though P9 mentions code tasks typically target 5-8 requirements overall—those are likely broader than just PRD sub-requirements.\n\nNow I need to flesh out the acceptance criteria to hit that 5-8 range. I'm mapping out specific, testable criteria like compilation without warnings, handling byte counts across the full scale from zero to petabytes, supporting both SI and binary unit systems, and I'll need a few more concrete acceptance criteria to round out the list.\n\nI've got the acceptance criteria solidified