 format package: size formatting for converting bytes to human-readable units, ANSI color code generation for file types and permissions, and column formatting utilities, along with the required reading materials the stitch agent will need to reference. I'm outlining the design for a Go format package with three core capabilities: human-readable size formatting supporting both SI and binary units, ANSI color codes for different file types, and column alignment for tabular output. The implementation will follow Go conventions by keeping functions independent of the sys package, accepting primitive types as inputs, and using the io.Writer interface where appropriate. I need to ensure the package passes linting checks and meets specific acceptance criteria around size conversion accuracy, correct ANSI escape sequences, and proper column alignment.\n\nLet me verify the design constraints are actually derived from the requirements rather than over-specified. The naming convention comes directly from the PRD, the separation from pkg/sys aligns with the architecture's role definitions, and using io.Writer follows Go standard library patterns. All three design decisions check out.\n\nI'm also validating the counts against the guidelines—six acceptance criteria fits the 5-8 target, three design decisions is within the 3-5 range, and three requirements respects the maximum of four PRD sub-requirements. There's a potential tension between the PRD's guidance on requirements and the constraint's limit, but I think the constraint is specifically about PRD-anchored requirements, so I'm interpreting this correctly.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering whether three requirements actually fits the scope. Each requirement roughly maps to one file and capability area: size formatting takes about 60-80 lines, ANSI colors need 60-100 lines, and column alignment requires 80-120 lines, which totals around 200-300 lines—potentially exceeding the 250-line target. I'm thinking about whether to defer column alignment to a second task since it's the most complex piece and primarily serves the ls command, while size formatting and colors are more immediately needed across multiple tools.\n\nBut dropping column alignment would leave me at only 120-180 lines, which feels too sparse. I'm going to keep all three because they're tightly integrated into the same use case, the 250-line limit is described as a target rather than a hard constraint, and splitting the format package further would feel over-engineered. The stitch agent can help keep things focused if the implementation runs long.\n\nI'm satisfied with this scope and ready to finalize the task definition.\n\nFor the title, I need to anchor to specific PRD requirements as P6 specifies, but I don't have access to the actual PRD content in the project context. The best I can do is reference the use case ID and let the stitch agent pull the specific requirement IDs from the PRD itself. My final title will be \"Implement pkg/format/ core formatting -- sizes, colors, columns (rel00.0-uc002-format)\".\n\nNow I'm second-guessing whether this is actually the right task to propose. Looking at the dependency chain, cat and sponge could technically be implemented with just testutils, which already exists. But P1 is clear about focusing on the earliest incomplete release from the roadmap, and that's release 00.0. So I should stick with the format task.\n\nI'm also thinking about whether I need to add a `doc.go` file for package documentation—it's standard Go practice for public packages, but the constraint says to avoid over-engineering. I'll keep it simple and put the package docs in one of the source files instead.\n\nSince the format package shouldn't depend on external libraries, there shouldn't be any changes needed to go.mod or go.sum. And on the human-readable size formatting, both SI and binary conversions are needed based on how tools like `du` work, so I'll make sure that's covered per the format PRD and let the implementation follow from there.\n\nNow let me verify my acceptance criteria are all checkable without ambiguity: the golangci-lint compilation check is straightforward, the byte conversion range is specific from 0 to petabyte scale, the ANSI color sequences are testable for the specific file types, column alignment can be validated with variable-width inputs, doc comments are verifiable, and the pkg/sys import restriction is clear. All of these are unambiguous and measurable.\n\nI'll structure my response by first summarizing the project state, then explaining the priority reasoning, and finally proposing the tasks in a YAML format.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":23741,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":23741},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c059ae7b-fe56-4ca4-b5c4-87f9e4390386","uuid":"dbd74e70-6e8f-4833-adcb-1e966ca9b0e5"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01QGxwzeP6PbZ5cFTyhZzexo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development. It derives formal specifications from C source code of Unix utilities (coreutils, moreutils, grep, findutils), generates Go implementations via an AI code generation pipeline (mage-claude-orchestrator), and verifies correctness through differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Key components:\n- `cmd/` — one Go package per utility\n- `pkg/testutils/` — differential testing harness (compares Go binary vs GNU binary)\n- `pkg/sys/` — Darwin/Linux syscall abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` — shared output formatting (column alignment, ANSI colors, human-readable sizes)\n- `magefiles/` — build targets (excluded from generation per DD7)\n- `docs/` — specifications (human-authored, read-only for the pipeline)\n\n**Implementation status**: `pkg/testutils/` exists in source code (difftest.go + tests). All other packages and utilities have no generated code on the working branch. Specs are complete for releases 00.0 through 04.1 (except 02.x which is pending).\n\n**Current release**: Release 00.0 (Batch 0 — foundational infrastructure) is the earliest incomplete release with 4 use cases: uc001-magefiles (excluded by DD7), uc002-format (pending), uc003-sys (pending), uc004-testutils (done — exists in source_code).\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nRelease 00.0 is the earliest incomplete release. Within it:\n- **uc001-magefiles**: Excluded from generation (DD7)\n- **uc004-testutils**: Already implemented (source_code shows difftest.go + difftest_test.go)\n- **uc002-format**: Needs implementation — alphabetically first remaining use case\n- **uc003-sys**: Needs implementation — after format\n\n`pkg/format/` and `pkg/sys/` are independent (format doesn't import sys). By P8's alphabetical tiebreaker, `pkg/format/` comes first. It's needed by `cmd/du` (rel01.3) for human-readable sizes and `cmd/ls` (rel04.0) for colors and column alignment.\n\nI propose one implementation task for `pkg/format/` core functionality. Tests will follow in a separate task per P2.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement pkg/format/ core formatting -- sizes, colors, columns (rel00.0-uc002-format)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (pkg/format/ component -- capabilities and design decisions)\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/ (locate the PRD for pkg/format -- glob for prd*format*.yaml)\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel00.0-uc002-format.yaml (use case for the format package)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (reference for package structure, copyright header, and code style)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/format/size.go\n        action: create\n        note: Human-readable size formatting (bytes to K/M/G/T/P units)\n      - path: pkg/format/color.go\n        action: create\n        note: ANSI color code generation for file types and permissions\n      - path: pkg/format/column.go\n        action: create\n        note: Column alignment for tabular output (ls -l style)\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement human-readable size formatting that converts byte counts to K/M/G/T/P units with appropriate precision per the format PRD. Support both 1024-based (binary) and 1000-based (SI) unit systems if the PRD requires both.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement ANSI color code output functions for file types (directory, symlink, executable, pipe, socket, block device, char device) and permission display per the format PRD.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement column alignment for tabular output that handles variable-width data and produces correctly padded columns for ls -l style display per the format PRD.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"One source file per capability, named after the primary exported type per project convention (P7). Follow the same copyright header and package comment style as pkg/testutils/difftest.go.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"No dependency on pkg/sys -- format functions accept primitive Go types (int64 for sizes, os.FileMode for colors, string slices for columns) as inputs, not file handles or paths.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Use io.Writer and fmt.Fprintf patterns for output generation where applicable, following Go standard library conventions.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"Package compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"Human-readable size formatting correctly converts byte counts from 0 through petabyte scale with appropriate unit selection and decimal precision.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"ANSI color functions produce correct escape sequences for all file types listed in R2 and reset sequences to terminate color output.\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"Column alignment produces correctly padded output when given variable-width input data across multiple columns.\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"All exported types and functions have Go doc comments following standard Go conventions.\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"pkg/format imports no packages from pkg/sys -- the package is self-contained for formatting logic.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":23741,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":23741},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c059ae7b-fe56-4ca4-b5c4-87f9e4390386","uuid":"fbf8b58b-1fc7-4ece-adcb-a97bc0a0c401"}
